d.\n\n- L3: (2,3), (4,1) maybe? Let's see slope = (1-3)/(4-2) = -2/2 = -1, not allowed. So not that. L3: (2,3) and (5,1) slope = (1-3)/(5-2) = -2/3, allowed. So L3 covers (2,3) and (5,1). Good.\n\n- L4: (2,4) and (4,2) maybe? Slope = (2-4)/(4-2) = -2/2 = -1, not allowed. L4: (2,4) and (3,1) slope = (1-4)/(3-2) = -3, allowed. So L4 covers (2,4) and (3,1). Good.\n\nNow we have covered column 2 points: (2,1) L1, (2,2) L2, (2,3) L3, (2,4) L4. Column 3 points: (3,1) L4, (3,2) L1, (3,3) L2. Column 4 points: (4,1) L3? Actually L3 covers (5,1) and (2,3). It does not cover (4,1). So (4,1) is not covered yet. Column 4 also has (4,2) not covered. Column 5 point (5,1) covered by L3. So we still need to cover (4,1) and (4,2). We have used all 4 sunny lines already (L1-L4). So we cannot cover (4,1) and (4,2). So this assignment fails.\n\nWe need to adjust assignments to also cover column 4 points. Perhaps we can assign some lines to include column 4 points as well. Since each line can have up to 4 points (one per column), we can try to assign each line to have points in columns 2,3,4,5. Let's try to find 4 lines that each have a point in each of columns 2-5 (except maybe some lines missing a column). Since we have 4 lines and 4 columns (2-5), we can try to assign each line to have exactly one point in each column, covering all points. That would be a perfect matching: each column's points are assigned to distinct lines. Since column 2 has 4 points, each line gets one. Column 3 has 3 points, so one line will not have a point in column 3. Column 4 has 2 points, so two lines will not have a point in column 4. Column 5 has 1 point, so three lines will not have a point in column 5. So we need to assign points accordingly.\n\nWe need to find lines L1-L4 such that:\n\n- L1: (2,1), (3,2), (4,3?) not needed, (5,4?) not needed. So maybe L1: (2,1),(3,2) as before.\n\n- L2: (2,2),(3,3) as before.\n\n- L3: (2,3),(4,1) maybe? But slope = (1-3)/(4-2) = -2/2 = -1, not allowed. So cannot pair (2,