2-3)/(3-2) = -1/1 = -1 => non-sunny. So not allowed.\n\nTry assign (2,1) to line A, (2,3) to line B, (3,2) to line A, (4,1) to line B. Then line A passes through (2,1) and (3,2) => slope 1 (sunny). Good. Line B passes through (2,3) and (4,1) => slope -1 (non-sunny). Not allowed.\n\nTry assign (2,1) to line A, (2,3) to line B, (3,2) to line B, (4,1) to line A. Then line A: (2,1) and (4,1) => slope 0 (non-sunny). Not allowed. So that fails.\n\nTry assign (2,1) to line A, (2,3) to line B, (3,2) to line A, (4,1) to line B. Then line A: (2,1) and (3,2) => slope 1 (sunny). Good. Line B: (2,3) and (4,1) => slope -1 (non-sunny). Not allowed.\n\nThus any assignment that pairs (2,3) with (4,1) yields slope -1. Could we avoid pairing those two points on same line? We need each line to have at most one point per column, but they can have more than two points. So we could have line B pass through (2,3) and (4,1) and maybe also (3,2) if that doesn't conflict? But (3,2) is column 3, line B currently has (2,3) column 2 and (4,1) column 4, so adding (3,2) would be column 3, okay. But line B would then have three points: (2,3),(3,2),(4,1). Let's see if there is a line passing through all three points. Check if they are collinear: (2,3),(3,2),(4,1) are indeed collinear because they lie on line x+y=5 (since 2+3=5,3+2=5,4+1=5). So they are collinear on line x+y=5, which is slope -1, non-sunny. So line B cannot be sunny.\n\nThus any line covering (2,3) and (4,1) must be slope -1, non-sunny. So we cannot have a sunny line covering both (2,3) and (4,1). So we need to assign them to different sunny lines. But we have only two sunny lines. So we need to assign (2,3) to line A, (4,1) to line B. Then we need to assign (2,1) and (3,2) to lines such that each line has at most one point per column. Since (2,3) uses column 2, line A cannot also have (2,1) because that would be two points in column 2. So (2,1) must go to line B. Then (3,2) is column 3, can go to either line, but line A currently ha